<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Maeatae</h1><p class="st-meta">180 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation just north of the Antonine Wall whose raids drew Septimius Severus to Scotland, and whose name survives in Dumyat near Stirling.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Maeatae are first named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, who describes them as living close to the wall that divided the island, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caledonii">Caledonians</a> beyond them. They seem to have been a grouping of peoples in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> area that emerged in the later 2nd century, perhaps including the earlier <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/venicones">Venicones</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Dio</a> portrays them, like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caledonii">Caledonians</a>, as living in tents, holding wives in common and fighting from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, a stereotype of barbarians rather than a reliable description. Around 197 the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virius-lupus">Virius Lupus</a> had to buy peace from them with large sums of money. The hill names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumyat">Dumyat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myot-hill">Myot Hill</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> are thought to preserve their name.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Renewed raids brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and from 208 to 211 he campaigned against the Maeatae and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caledonii">Caledonii</a>. The Maeatae revolted again in 210. After Severus died at York, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> withdrew, and by about 300 the northern peoples were known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, later forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/maeatae.jpg" alt="Maeatae" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Doug Lee · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:From_Dumyat_looking_towards_Grangemouth_(geograph_3766267).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>180 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic (Pictish)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
